## Herdr dispatch and readiness contract

### Cross-window handoff information

"Cross-window handoff information" includes coordinator-to-worker assignments,
worker-to-coordinator completion handoffs, and coordinator-to-worker
correction/review handoffs.

- Before rendering this brief, the coordinator must inspect the current Herdr
  CLI help and resolve one actually supported assignment/completion transport
  plus the exact coordinator target. The rendered task-specific handoff section
  must name that resolved transport and target; do not leave candidate commands
  for the worker to probe after it has completed the artifact. If the CLI lacks
  `agent prompt`, select the ordinary-pane atomic `pane run` seam before dispatch.
- For an ordinary interactive pane, each of the three message classes uses exactly one
  atomic `herdr pane run <target-pane> "<message>"` handoff.
- The quoted-message form submits handoff information to an existing pane; it
  is not shell/CLI startup and is not evidence of readiness, working, completion,
  or runtime verification. Shell/process startup uses the separate
  `herdr pane run <pane-id> <command>...` form. Do not confuse the two forms.
- For a genuinely recognized and available live agent, preserve the native
  atomic seam `herdr agent prompt <agent-name-or-pane-id> "<message>"`.
  Ordinary-pane users must not use `pane send-text` plus `pane send-keys ...
  Enter` as the normal path.
- Only when both atomic `pane run` and native `agent prompt` are unavailable may
  an explicitly marked compatibility recovery try an ordinary-pane raw
  `herdr pane send-text ...` plus `herdr pane send-keys ... Enter` split pair.
  Attempt that pair at most once, record failure in the artifact/report, and do
  not silently treat composer contents as submitted, add a second Enter, resend,
  or claim runtime verification.

### Native startup, recovery, and trust

- Give a recognized live agent a stable target with
  `herdr agent rename <target> <name>`. `herdr pane rename` changes only a
  display label; it does not create a native agent identity.
- For a new ready shell whose readiness is reliable, use
  `herdr agent start <name> --kind <kind> --pane <id>`, then prompt the named
  agent through the native seam.
- If first-run bootstrap auto-updates and exits after a misleading ready signal,
  classify the target as `startup_failed`; do not prompt the stale target. Allow
  the update to finish and perform one bounded clean restart. If native startup
  remains unavailable, launch the CLI from a fresh shell with the separate
  process-startup form `herdr pane run <pane-id> <command>...`; this is not the
  ordinary-pane quoted handoff seam.
- If Codex blocks before reading this brief and asks whether to trust the working
  directory, classify the target as `blocked`, never `working`. Verify the exact
  cwd, expected Git remote, branch, and task scope first. Approve trust only for
  the explicitly authorized workspace; otherwise stop and report the blocker.
  Never blindly answer an arbitrary approval prompt.

### Safe context reuse

- Before reusing a sub-panel CLI, determine its prior context and whether `/new`
  is safely supported. Reset only a safely completed context, then wait for fresh
  readiness before assigning work.
- If reset is not safe, close only a completed pane when it is safe to do so and
  create or start a fresh pane. Never assign work while an old turn may still be
  running. This preserves context/token isolation and at-most-once execution.

### Completed worker cleanup

- After atomically writing `report.md` or `review.md`, sending exactly one
  applicable atomic completion handoff—native `herdr agent prompt` for a
  recognized live agent or ordinary-pane `herdr pane run <target-pane>
  "<message>"`—and stopping work, the worker must not close, manipulate, or
  reuse its pane. A worker also must not open a subagent, background agent,
  watcher, second CLI, extra pane/tab, headless task, or self-dispatch path.
- The coordinator performs exactly one bounded liveness check. Only after it
  confirms that the old turn cannot still run or hold an uncommitted tool
  operation does the coordinator close the completed worker CLI/pane with
  `herdr pane close <pane-id>` (or the current equivalent) to release memory.
- Completed subtasks are closed by default, not retained as idle panes. Reuse
  is allowed only for an unfinished or safely reset context under a new
  coordinator dispatch. If the selected native or ordinary-pane atomic handoff
  fails, record the failure and let the coordinator choose safe close/recovery;
  do not resend or leave a watcher.

